{
 "schema": 1,
 "note": "What the outlets of a mall sell (ECO-013, ECO-014; ADR-0041; the owner, 2026-11-24: \"each outlets should have buyable products in it with decent catalog ... some of these object can be placed in the house like guitar, books etc. and some like clothes will be made available in wardrobe\"). 'sells': for each kind of outlet in mall_outlets.json, the goods that kind deals in; one outlet sells 'per_outlet' of them, chosen by a fixed hash of its unit id, so two shops of a kind differ. An id in 'sells' is a good defined here or an item of economy.json (bread, coffee, the novel). 'goods': each good's name, its usual price in chips (100 chips = 1 brix) and where it goes when bought ('goes'): 'carry' it is carried, and can be sold on at a market stall; 'eat' food and drink, carried like a cafe's (there are no needs to eat for yet); 'home' a piece of World/rules/furnishing.json ('piece'), which goes to the belongings and is placed in a home from the building designer's Furnish tab; 'wear' clothes, which go to the wardrobe: 'parts' are the parts of the look (settings.json wardrobe.parts) it sets when put on. 'stock': how many of each good an outlet holds at each restock (economy.json stock.restock_minute); its price follows its stock by economy.json 'price', as a street shop's does. Outlets buy nothing back. Every name, price and number is a first guess for play-testing [ASSUMPTION]. Rules: Tools/citysim/economy.py; checked by world.mall_goods_problems().",
 "per_outlet": 8,
 "stock": { "target": 6 },
 "goes": {
  "carry": "Carried; can be set out on a market stall.",
  "eat": "Food and drink; carried, as a cafe's is.",
  "home": "A piece for the home: to the belongings, placed from the Furnish tab.",
  "wear": "Clothes: to the wardrobe."
 },
